<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durgadas Rathore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1638">1638</a>–<a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durgadas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> was a Rathore noble of Marwar who led resistance to the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Jaswant Singh in 1678. He protected the infant heir Ajit Singh and fought a long war until Ajit Singh regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> in 1707. He is celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> as a model of loyalty and courage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durgadas-rathore.jpg" alt="Durgadas Rathore" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archibald Herman Muller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durgadas_Rathore_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Durgadas was born on 13 August 1638, the son of Askaran Rathore, a jagirdar of Drunera and a minister under Jaswant Singh, the ruler of Marwar. He was a distant relative of the royal family, descended from Karana, a son of Rao Ranmal. When Jaswant Singh died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in December 1678 without an heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> imposed direct rule over Marwar. Two of the late ruler's queens then gave birth to sons, one of whom died soon after, leaving Ajit Singh as the sole heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> had the infant Ajit Singh brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and held under house arrest at Noorgarh. Durgadas and other nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> attacked the Mughal contingent in Delhi and rescued the child and the widowed queens. Ajit Singh was hidden first at Balunda, then in the Aravalli Hills near Abu Sirohi, and later at Nandlai in Mewar, where Rana Raj Singh I offered him refuge. Tahir Beg and Inder Singh Rathore, sent to capture Durgadas, both failed.</p><p>Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare Durgadas harassed Mughal outposts and made Mughal officers pay tribute. He encouraged the princes Muazzam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> against their father, and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s granddaughter Saif-un-nissa in his care before returning her. After Aurangzeb's death in 1707 he and Ajit Singh seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>. In the Rajput Rebellion of 1708 to 1710 he routed Saiyid Hussain Khan Barha at Kaladera in July 1708 and helped win the victory at Sambhar alongside Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Jai Singh II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ajit Singh grew jealous of his fame, and Durgadas turned against him after Ajit Singh had the loyal noble Mukund Das murdered. Durgadas was exiled and his name erased from the Marwari chronicles, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> offered him the title of Rao, and Maharana Amar Singh II of Mewar gave him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> of Rampura and Vijaypur. He died on the banks of the Shipra River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> on 22 November 1718. The historian Jadunath Sarkar praised his honour and constancy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postage-stamp">postage stamp</a> in his memory in 1988 and coins in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durgadas Rathore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Rathore princes became Mughal commanders, but when Aurangzeb tried to take Marwar after Jaswant Singh's death in 1678, <b>Durgadas Rathore</b> led a long resistance.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.18,75.78&amp;year=1690" aria-label="Ujjain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Durgadas Rathore"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3893 5513h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3893 5513h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=23.18,75.78&amp;year=1690">What was here in 1690? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
